Given vector field \[\vec{f}=y\hat{i}+x\hat{j}+z\hat{k}.\] Using Gauss divergence theorem, \[\iint_S \vec{f}\cdot d\vec{s}
=
\iiint_V (\nabla\cdot \vec{f})\,dV.\] Now, \[\nabla\cdot \vec{f}
=
\frac{\partial y}{\partial x}
+
\frac{\partial x}{\partial y}
+
\frac{\partial z}{\partial z}\] \[=0+0+1=1.\] The given surface is a cube with vertices from \[(0,0,0)\ \text{to}\ (1,1,1),\] hence side length \[=1.\] Therefore, \[\text{Volume of cube}=1^3=1.\] Thus, \[\iint_S \vec{f}\cdot d\vec{s}
=
\iiint_V 1\,dV
=1.\] \[\boxed{1}\]

The solution must be inferred from the given graph. <img src="img/420044f2f3af371b.webp" loading="lazy" alt="" style="width:min(100%,420px)"> From the graph, the curve is represented by \[y = \begin{cases}
    \dfrac{x^2}{2}, &amp; x\ge 0,\\ -\dfrac{x^2}{2}, &amp; x&lt;0.
\end{cases}\] <div class="sol-block sol-method"><div class="sol-block-title">Method 1 (Piecewise Differentiation)</div>Differentiating each branch, \[\frac{dy}{dx} = \begin{cases}
    x, &amp; x\ge 0,\\ -x, &amp; x&lt;0.
\end{cases}\] Since \[|x| = \begin{cases}
    x, &amp; x\ge 0,\\ -x, &amp; x&lt;0,
\end{cases}\] we obtain \[\frac{dy}{dx} = |x|.\]</div> <div class="sol-block sol-method"><div class="sol-block-title">Method 2 (Graph Interpretation)</div>Observe that the slope of the curve is always non-negative. For \(x&gt;0\), \[y = \frac{x^2}{2}\quad\Rightarrow\quad\frac{dy}{dx} = x\] For \(x&lt;0\), \[y = -\frac{x^2}{2}\quad\Rightarrow\quad\frac{dy}{dx} = -x\] Combining both regions, \[\frac{dy}{dx} = |x|.\]</div> \[\boxed{\text{(D)}\ \frac{dy}{dx} = |x|}\] <div class="sol-block sol-keypoints"><div class="sol-block-title">Key points</div><ul><li>Differentiating piecewise yields the absolute value function.</li></ul></div> <div class="sol-block sol-mistakes"><div class="sol-block-title">Mistakes to avoid</div><ul><li>Do not choose \(\dfrac{dy}{dx}=x\) by looking only at the right half of the graph.</li></ul></div>

The input bias current is the average of the two input bias currents: \[I_{B}=\frac{I_{b1}+I_{b2}}{2}
=\frac{450+350}{2}
=400\,\text{nA}\] The input offset current is the difference between the two input bias currents: \[I_{f}=|I_{b1}-I_{b2}|
=|450-350|
=100\,\text{nA}\] Thus, \[\boxed{\text{(d) }I_{B}=400\,\text{nA},\; I_{f}=100\,\text{nA}}\] <div class="sol-block sol-keypoints"><div class="sol-block-title">Key points</div><ul><li>Input bias current: \[I_{B}=\frac{I_{b1}+I_{b2}}{2}\]</li><li>Input offset current: \[I_{f}=|I_{b1}-I_{b2}|\]</li><li>Ideally, both \(I_{B}\) and \(I_{f}\) are zero.</li></ul></div>

The capacitor voltage is the input to the Schmitt trigger. When \(V_{o}=5\,\text{V}\), the capacitor charges from \(2\,\text{V}\) to \(3\,\text{V}\) through \[R=10\,\text{k}\Omega,\qquad C=1\,\mu\text{F}\] with \[RC=10\,\text{ms}.\] Charging equation: \[v_{C}(t)=5-(5-2)e^{-t/RC}.\] At the switching instant, \[3=5-3e^{-t/RC}\] \[e^{-t/RC}=\frac{2}{3}\] \[t_{1}=RC\ln\!\left(\frac{3}{2}\right).\] Similarly, when \(V_{o}=0\,\text{V}\), the capacitor discharges from \(3\,\text{V}\) to \(2\,\text{V}\): \[v_{C}(t)=3e^{-t/RC}.\] At the next switching instant, \[2=3e^{-t/RC}\] \[t_{2}=RC\ln\!\left(\frac{3}{2}\right).\] Hence, the time period is \[T=t_{1}+t_{2}
=2RC\ln\!\left(\frac{3}{2}\right)
=2(10)\ln\!\left(\frac{3}{2}\right)\,\text{ms}\] \[T\approx 8.11\,\text{ms}.\] \[\boxed{8.11}\]

The given circuit in question is a second-order Sallen--Key low-pass filter. <img src="img/72fe8081fa9ee400.webp" loading="lazy" alt="" style="width:min(100%,288px)"> The cutoff frequency is \[f_{c}=\frac{1}{2\pi\sqrt{R_{1}R_{2}C_{1}C_{2}}}.\] Given, \[R_{1}=R_{2}=22\,\text{k}\Omega,\qquad
C_{1}=C_{2}=0.47\,\mu\text{F}.\] Since the components are equal, \[f_{c}=\frac{1}{2\pi RC}.\] Substituting the values, \[f_{c}
=\frac{1}{2\pi(22\times10^{3})(0.47\times10^{-6})}\] \[=\frac{1}{0.06497}
\approx 15.4\,\text{Hz}.\] (The resistors \(91\,\text{k}\Omega\) and \(82\,\text{k}\Omega\) set the amplifier gain and do not affect the cutoff frequency.) \[\boxed{15.4}\]

For the transistor \(T_{1}\), \[I_{C}+2I_{B}=\frac{9-V_{BE}}{5\,\text{k}\Omega}
=\frac{9-0.7}{5\,\text{k}\Omega}
=1.66\,\text{mA}\] Using \[I_{B}=\frac{I_{C}}{\beta}, \qquad \beta=100\] \[I_{C}\left(1+\frac{2}{100}\right)=1.66\] \[I_{C}
=\frac{1.66}{1+\frac{2}{100}}
=1.62\,\text{mA}\] Since \(T_{1}\) and \(T_{2}\) are identical, \[I_{C2}=1.62\,\text{mA}\] The current through the \(3\,\text{k}\Omega\) resistor is therefore \(1.62\,\text{mA}\). Hence, \[V_{1}-V_{2}
=
V_{BE}+(3\,\text{k}\Omega)(1.62\,\text{mA})\] \[V_{1}-V_{2}
=
0.7+4.86
=
5.56\approx5.58\,\text{V}\] \[\boxed{5.58}\] <div class="sol-block sol-keypoints"><div class="sol-block-title">Key points</div><ul><li>The diode-connected transistor \(T_{1}\) sets the reference current.</li><li>For matched transistors, \(I_{C1}=I_{C2}\).</li></ul></div>

Let \(L_p\) be the length of the platform and \(v\) be the speed of the train. Length of the train \(L_t = 280\,\text{m}\). 1. Crossing the platform: \[v = \frac{L_t + L_p}{60} = \frac{280 + L_p}{60}\] 2. Passing the man: \[v = \frac{L_t}{20} = \frac{280}{20} = 14\,\text{m/s}\] 3. Equating speed: \[\begin{aligned}
    14 &amp;= \frac{280 + L_p}{60} \\
    840 &amp;= 280 + L_p \\
    L_p &amp;= 560\,\text{m}
\end{aligned}\] \[\boxed{560}\] <div class="sol-block sol-keypoints"><div class="sol-block-title">Key points</div><ul><li><strong>Relative Motion:</strong> When a train crosses a platform, the total distance covered is the sum of the lengths of the train and the platform.</li></ul></div>

To determine the probability that the batch is accepted, we analyze the sampling process: <ul><li>Total bulbs (\(N\)) = 100</li><li>Non-defective bulbs (\(G\)) = 95</li><li>Sample size (\(n\)) = 4</li></ul> The batch is accepted only if all 4 bulbs chosen are non-defective. Since the bulbs are chosen from a finite lot, this follows a hypergeometric distribution (sampling without replacement). <strong>Probability Calculation:</strong><br/>The probability is the ratio of the number of ways to choose 4 non-defective bulbs to the total number of ways to choose any 4 bulbs: \[P(\text{Accepted}) = \dfrac{^{95}C_{4}}{^{100}C_{4}}\] Expanding the combinations down to their individual structural components: \[\begin{aligned}
P(\text{Accepted}) &amp;= \dfrac{\left( \dfrac{95!}{4! \times 91!} \right)}{\left( \dfrac{100!}{4! \times 96!} \right)} \\[1.5ex]
&amp;= \dfrac{95 \times 94 \times 93 \times 92}{100 \times 99 \times 98 \times 97} \\[1.5ex]
&amp;= \dfrac{95}{100} \times \dfrac{94}{99} \times \dfrac{93}{98} \times \dfrac{92}{97}
\end{aligned}\] Solving the numerical expression: \[P(\text{Accepted}) \approx 0.8118\] \[\boxed{0.81}\] <div class="sol-block sol-keypoints"><div class="sol-block-title">Key points</div><ul><li><strong>Hypergeometric Distribution</strong>: This method is mandatory when the sample size is a significant portion of a finite population and sampling occurs without replacement.</li></ul></div>
